.\" hello world
.\"
.\" The manual page. Read it where manuals are read:
.\"
.\"   curl -s https://helloworldandthen.com/world.1 | man -l -
.\"   man ./world.1
.\"
.\" Lines that begin with a full stop are instructions to the typesetter and
.\" are not shown. On this world a full stop is night ocean, so the manual is
.\" held up, all the way down its left-hand side, by a column of deep water.
.\" You are reading the part that is under the surface.
.\"
.TH WORLD 1 "whenever it is looked at" "hello world" "Provisional Commands"
.SH NAME
world \- say hello, and then turn
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B world
.RB [ still ]
.br
.B world
.RB [ \-\-help ]
.RI [ anything\ else ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B world
writes
.I hello world
once, at the top, and does not take it back.
It then draws a ball in characters and turns it eastward, about once in nine
seconds if left alone, until the visitor goes away.
The visitor going away is not detected.
.PP
Land is drawn where the land is and sea where it is not.
Which is which was decided before the first visitor and is not configurable.
The light comes from one corner.
A moon, the At, goes round in front and round behind, and is drawn only while
it is in front, this being a limitation of the medium and of moons.
.PP
One ship is included.
It is not started by
.B world
and cannot be stopped by it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B still
Hold the world for one picture and leave.
This is also what happens when the output is a file, a pipe or a printer.
The world goes on turning; only the picture is still.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Accepted.
No help is available, there being nobody who has been.
See
.BR phrasebook (7)
for how to ask.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Ignored.
The world has said two words and regards the matter as closed.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Already the default below latitude sixty south.
.TP
.BR \-n ,\  \-\-dry\-run
Not supported.
About seven tenths of it is wet and will stay so.
.TP
.BR \-f ,\  \-\-force
Has no effect on the tide, which has its own.
.TP
.BR \-r ,\  \-\-reverse
Turn it westward.
This can be done by hand and has been.
The visitor responsible knows.
.TP
.BR \-v ,\  \-\-version
Prints nothing.
There is one world and it has not been numbered, in case that should seem to
invite another.
.SH OPERANDS
None are required.
Flags may be planted, nine at most; a tenth is accepted and the oldest falls
over without a diagnostic.
A bottle may be supplied.
It is passed along unread.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LANG
Not consulted.
The surface is punctuation throughout and reads the same in every locale.
.TP
.B TERM
If the terminal is cold, see
.BR pattern (5).
.TP
.B TZ
Not consulted.
It is whatever time the Hash is passing over.
.TP
.B HOME
Longitude nought.
The world may be put back there.
It does not stay.
.TP
.B EDITOR
Unset.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /
The world.
.TP
.I /dev/null
Elsewhere.
A great deal is written there and none of it is listed.
.TP
.I ~
The Tilde Sea.
On other systems this is where a user lives.
Here nobody does, and it has been left as open water in full sun.
.TP
.I /tmp
The Exclamations.
.TP
.I /lost+found
The Caret, from time to time.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B world
does not exit.
A status of 0 is reported by the visitor's own machine when the visitor
stops, and should be read as a remark about the visitor.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B "hello world"
Not an error.
It is the only message, and it is entered here so that the list should
contain one.
.TP
.B "No such file or directory"
You are in Elsewhere.
The way back is marked.
.TP
.B "Permission denied"
Never issued.
There is a desk, but nobody is behind it.
.TP
.B "Broken pipe"
The weather was sent down one.
See
.BR BUGS .
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGINT
The visitor stops.
The world is told goodbye and does not answer.
.TP
.B SIGSTOP
Equivalent to space.
The world holds still and may be let go.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Not delivered.
The Caret has never had a line to hang up.
.TP
.B SIGWINCH
The window has changed size.
The world has not.
.PP
Signals made with two arms on a mast are a separate facility and are
documented by the mast.
.SH BUGS
None have been found.
The Commas were reported as one and the report was closed as
.IR "life, unconfirmed" .
.PP
Weather cannot be piped.
.PP
The moon has one side.
This is by design and is shared with a neighbouring implementation.
.PP
Clouds at night are neither drawn nor not drawn.
A fix would require somebody standing under them.
.PP
The ship cannot be addressed, steered, hailed or passed as an argument.
Reports that it has been seen to turn towards land have not been reproduced.
.PP
There is no
.B undo
for the first two words.
None is planned.
.SH CAVEATS
This page documents a single copy.
Yours turned differently.
.SH AUTHORS
Nobody.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR caret (8),
.BR at (1),
.BR forecast (5),
.BR phrasebook (7),
.BR pattern (5),
.BR elsewhere (2),
.BR nobody (0)
.PP
Of these only
.BR at (1)
is likely to be installed, and it is a different one.
It will offer to do something for you later.
The At makes no such offer, and is on time.
